4. `src/App.css` — small layout styles using `--g-*` tokens

Let me design the grouped table carefully:

```tsx
// data/orders.ts
export type OrderStatus = 'new' | 'processing' | 'shipped' | 'delivered' | 'cancelled';

export interface Order {
  id: string;
  number: string;
  customer: string;
  city: string;
  createdAt: string; // ISO date string for display
  amount: number;
  status: OrderStatus;
}

export interface OrderGroup {
  id: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  items: Order[];
}

export type OrderTableItem = OrderGroup | Order;

export const isOrderGroup = (item: OrderTableItem): item is OrderGroup => 'items' in item;

export const STATUS_ORDER: OrderStatus[] = ['new', 'processing', 'shipped', 'delivered', 'cancelled'];

export const STATUS_META: Record<OrderStatus, {title: string; theme: 'info'|'warning'|'utility'|'success'|'danger'}> = {
  new: {title: 'Новый', theme: 'info'},
  processing: {title: 'В обработке', theme: 'warning'},
  shipped: {title: 'Отправлен', theme: 'utility'},
  delivered: {title: 'Доставлен', theme: 'success'},
  cancelled: {title: 'Отменён', theme: 'danger'},
};
```

Generate ~48 orders deterministically (no Math.random — stable renders; actually random at module scope is fine but deterministic is better for StrictMode double render consistency... module scope computed once, fine either way; I'll write a seeded generator).

Grouping builder:
```ts
export const groupOrdersByStatus = (orders: Order[]): OrderTableItem[] =>
  STATUS_ORDER.map(status => ({
    id: `status-${status}`,
    status,
    items: orders.filter(o => o.status === status),
  })).filter(g => g.items.length > 0);
```

OrdersTable.tsx:

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import {ColumnReorderingProvider, Table, TreeExpandableCell, selectionColumn, useRowSelectionFixedHandler, useTable} from '@gravity-ui/table';
import type {ColumnDef, ExpandedState, RowSelectionState} from '@gravity-ui/table/tanstack';
import {Label, Text} from '@gravity-ui/uikit';
import {isOrderGroup, STATUS_META, type Order, type OrderTableItem} from '../data/orders';
```

Wait — `@gravity-ui/table/tanstack` subpath exports ColumnDef etc. — confirmed by docs ("Types come from the @gravity-ui/table/tanstack subpath") and exports keys include './tanstack'. 

Columns:
```tsx
const columns: ColumnDef<OrderTableItem>[] = [
  selectionColumn as ColumnDef<OrderTableItem>,
  {
    accessorKey: 'number',
    header: 'Заказ',
    size: 260,
    withNestingStyles: true,
    cell: (info) => {
      const item = info.row.original;
      if (isOrderGroup(item)) return STATUS_META[item.status].title; // for group row... 
      return (
        <TreeExpandableCell row={info.row}>{item.number}</TreeExpandableCell>
      );
    },
  },
  ...
];
```

Hmm — how do group rows render? In the docs' GroupingExample, group rows are rendered by the Table as group header rows (via getGroupTitle) — not through cells. But `TreeExpandableCell` in the docs is used for the column that should show expand/collapse control, applied on leaf rows' cells... Actually in docs example 2, the cell wraps `TreeExpandableCell` around the value for the name column — for rows that can expand (parents), TreeExpandableCell renders the expand arrow; for leaves just content. But if the parent row is rendered as a full-width group header, the expand control is in the group header itself.

Look at docs again: GroupingExample passes `getGroupTitle` to `<Table>` and doesn't use TreeExpandableCell. The Table detects group rows (rows with subRows, presumably) and renders them as group headers with title + expand control. Then separately "To add a control for expanding/collapsing rows, wrap the cell content with TreeExpandableCell" — that's an alternative/complement.

I'll follow the main GroupingExample: getGroupTitle + enableExpanding + getSubRows. The first data column gets `withNestingStyles: true`? That adds nesting indent styles to cells — good for leaves under groups. Let me keep it simple and follow docs:

```tsx
const columns = [
  selectionColumn as ColumnDef<OrderTableItem>,
  {accessorKey: 'number', header: '№ заказа', size: 140, withNestingStyles: true},
  {accessorKey: 'customer', header: 'Клиент', size: 220},
  {accessorKey: 'city', header: 'Город', size: 160},
  {accessorKey: 'createdAt', header: 'Дата', size: 130},
  {accessorKey: 'amount', header: 'Сумма', size: 140, cell: ...},
  {accessorKey: 'status', header: 'Статус', size: 160, cell: ...},
];
```

For amount cell: `info.getValue()` on group rows — accessorKey 'amount' doesn't exist on OrderGroup → undefined. Cell renderers: guard `if (!info.row.original || isOrderGroup(info.row.original)) return null;` — but if group rows are rendered as group headers, cells never render for them. Still, guard defensively for amount/status cells since group rows might render cells in some path... Actually wait — do group rows render as full-width headers automatically? BaseRow has `getIsGroupHeaderRow` prop — there must be a default implementation. Let me check the default: probably `row.getCanExpand()`? Let me quickly grep BaseRow to be sure, because it affects whether cells render for group rows.

Also `getGroupTitle` signature check.

